{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And Moab said to the elders of Midian.</b> Now were they not enemies, one to the other, as it says (Judges 5:?) — but as for the verse cited here, it is said elsewhere. Rather, it is written (Numbers — 'those who strike Midian in the field of Moab') — that is, Midian came against Moab to war; yet from their fear of Israel they made peace between themselves. A parable: two dogs that were snarling at each other; a wolf came upon one of them. He said in his heart, 'If I do not help him today, he kills this one and tomorrow comes upon me'; the two of them joined together against the wolf. And why did they take counsel from Midian? Because they saw that Israel were victorious not in the way of the world, they said: their leader was raised in Midian — let us ask what his measure is. They told them: his power is only in his mouth. They said: we too will come against them with a man whose power is only in his mouth. For they supposed that Moses our master, peace be upon him, was like the rest of astrologers."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "The comment opens with a citation marked '(שם לו)' referring to a verse about Midian and Moab; the precise reference is uncertain. The prooftext about striking Midian appears to allude to Numbers or Judges material. Rendered hyper-literally; the citation resolution remains uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:34:52.992331869Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}